{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-314895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An environmental researcher is tracking the depth $d$, in meters, of a lake during a dry season. The following table shows the rate at which the depth is decreasing.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(weeks)}$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${d}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{(meters/week)}$ | $-0.3$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.8$ | $-1.0$ |\n\nAt $t=2,$ the lake depth was $12$ meters. Use the tangent line approximation of $d$ at time $t=2$ to estimate the depth of the lake at $t=2.4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-314895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $d(t)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =d(a)+{d}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =d(2)+{d}^{′}(2)(t-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $d(2.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=2.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(2.4) & ≈L(2.4) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(2.4-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(0.4) \\\\  & =12-0.2 \\\\  & =11.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be $11.8$ meters." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-314895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-314895" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-314895" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11.8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An engineer measures the amount of fuel $F$, in liters, remaining in a generator. The following table shows the rate at which fuel is being consumed.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(hours)}$** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{(liters/hour)}$ | $-3.1$ | $-3.5$ | $-4.0$ | $-4.4$ |\n\nAt $t=4,$ the generator contained $62$ liters of fuel. Use the tangent line approximation of $F$ at time $t=4$ to estimate the amount of fuel remaining at $t=4.6.$  Do not round the answer.\n\nThe amount of fuel remaining at $t=4.6$ hours is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nliters." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $F(t)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =F(a)+{F}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =F(4)+{F}^{′}(4)(t-4) \\\\  & =62-3.5(t-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $F(4.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=4.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4.6) & ≈L(4.6) \\\\  & =62-3.5(4.6-4) \\\\  & =62-3.5(0.6) \\\\  & =62-2.1 \\\\  & =59.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe amount of fuel remaining at $t=4.6$ hours is estimated to be $59.9$ liters." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-346048" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346048" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "59.9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the function $f(x)$ passes through $(1,-4)$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=2.$ Determine $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n$L(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the graph of $f(x)$ passes through $(1,-4)$, we have that $f(1)=-4.$ Therefore, the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $a=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =-4+2(x-1) \\\\  & =-4+2x-2 \\\\  & =2x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-346036" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346036" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(-4)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(-4)=6.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-4,$ estimate the value of $f(-3.8).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(-3.8)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-4)+{f}^{′}(-4)(x-(-4)) \\\\  & =1+6(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-3.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-3.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3.8) & ≈L(-3.8) \\\\  & =1+6(-3.8+4) \\\\  & =1+6(0.2) \\\\  & =1+1.2 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-346041" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346041" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the function $f(x)$ passes through $(2,-1)$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=4.$ Determine $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\n$L(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the graph of $f(x)$ passes through $(2,-1)$, we have that $f(2)=-1.$ Therefore, the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =-1+4(x-2) \\\\  & =-1+4x-8 \\\\  & =4x-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-346037" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346037" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x-9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=(2x-3)^{2}(x-1)^{4}$ and $f(0)=-2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ to estimate the value of $f(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(0)=-2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)==(2x-3)^{2}(x-1)^{4},$ then ${f}^{′}(0)==(2(0)-3)^{2}(0-1)^{4}=9.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-2+9x.\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =-2+9(1) \\\\  & =-2+9 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-4870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(4x-9)$ and $f(1)=-2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(1.5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)=-2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(4x-9),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)={1}^{2}(4\\cdot 1-9)=-5.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-2-5(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.5)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.5) & ≈L(1.5) \\\\  & =-2-5(1.5-1) \\\\  & =-2-5(0.5) \\\\  & =-2-2.5 \\\\  & =-4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-115608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-14.5"] ["math" "-4.5"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "12.5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4.5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ and $f(2)=4.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ to estimate the value of $f(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=4,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2x,$ then ${f}^{′}(2)=2(2)=4.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=4+4(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ≈L(3) \\\\  & =4+4(3-2) \\\\  & =4+4(1) \\\\  & =4+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-4813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "8"] ["math" "8.5"] ["math" "8.75"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "9.25"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4813" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-314900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-314900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{\\pi }{5})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & ≈L(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{30} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-314900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-314900" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-314900" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt{3}}{120}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-4$ and $f(4)=13.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(4)=13,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-4,$ then ${f}^{′}(4)={4}^{2}-4=12.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=13+12(x-4).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3.5)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.5) & ≈L(3.5) \\\\  & =13+12(3.5-4) \\\\  & =13+12(-0.5) \\\\  & =13-6 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-4804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5.5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "6.5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-4804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-48404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(3,-3)$ and is concave down on the interval $(1,4).$ If ${f}^{′}(3)=4,$ estimate the value of $f(3.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-48404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(3,-3),$ so we have $f(3)=-3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(3)=4,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-3+4(x-3) \\\\  & =-3+4x-12 \\\\  & =4x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.2) & ≈L(3.2) \\\\  & =4(3.2)-15 \\\\  & =12.8-15 \\\\  & =-2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=3,$ and therefore we have an overestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-48404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-48404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-48404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(3.2)≈-2.2,$ an overestimate"] ["text" "$f(3.2)≈-2.2,$ an underestimate"] ["text" "$f(3.2)≈-2.6,$ an overestimate"] ["text" "$f(3.2)≈-2.6,$ an underestimate"] ["text" "$f(3.2)≈-3.4,$ an overestimate"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-48404" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(3.2)≈-2.2,$ an overestimate"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-3,-4)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2).$ If ${f}^{′}(-3)=3,$ estimate the value of $f(-2.9)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-3$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-3)+{f}^{′}(-3)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(-3,-4),$ so we have $f(-3)=-4.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(-3)=3,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-4+3(x+3) \\\\  & =-4+3x+9 \\\\  & =3x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.9)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.9.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.9) & ≈L(-2.9) \\\\  & =3(-2.9)+5 \\\\  & =-8.7+5 \\\\  & =-3.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-3,$ and therefore we have an overestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-115269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(-2.9)≈-3.7,$ an overestimate"] ["text" "$f(-2.9)≈-3.7,$ an underestimate"] ["text" "$f(-2.9)≈-4.3,$ an overestimate"] ["text" "$f(-2.9)≈-4.3,$ an underestimate"] ["text" "$f(-2.9)≈-4.6,$ an underestimate"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(-2.9)≈-3.7,$ an overestimate"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-115261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)$ has a global minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$f(x)$ is a linear function"] ["text" "$f(x)$ is concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$f(x)$ is concave up in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-115261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)$ is concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-47782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(2,3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0,3).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=-1,$ estimate the value of $f(2.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-47782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(2,3),$ so we have $f(2)=3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(2)=-1,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+(-1)(x-2) \\\\  & =3-(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.2) & ≈L(2.2) \\\\  & =3-(2.2-2) \\\\  & =3-0.2 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(0,3),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=2,$ and therefore we have an underestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-47782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-47782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/r