asses through the point $(2,1),$ so we have $f(2)=1.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(2)=1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =1+1(x-2) \\\\  & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.6) & ≈L(1.6) \\\\  & =1.6-1 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=2,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "70f48a39bf56b17c83bbb883a4c4b959255261aef762584965a335872cbf805e"} {"question" "q-346718" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346718" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.2"] ["math" "0.6"] ["math" "1.2"] ["math" "1.6"] ["math" "2.0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346718" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346718" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346718" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "overestimate"] ["text" "underestimate"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "q-346718" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "overestimate"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c4716e6b3746fbc0554b6715aa4b2425057728391d9d23ad17afe780a034f63"} {"question" "e-291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(-2) = 0$ and $f'(-2)=2.$ Estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/example-291.json" "file_sha256" "5540ed482bbf9ef14b0f95e5474b6e975d21be2b9263d2c7ee0687c95baf114e"} {"question" "e-291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =0+2(x+2) \\\\  & =2(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.8) & ≈L(-1.8) \\\\  & =2(-1.8+2) \\\\  & =2(0.2) \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/example-291.json" "file_sha256" "5540ed482bbf9ef14b0f95e5474b6e975d21be2b9263d2c7ee0687c95baf114e"} {"question" "e-292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $g$ be a function such that $g'(x) =\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(4) = 3.$ Using the tangent line approximation of $g(x)$ at $x=4,$ estimate the value of $g(3.6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/example-292.json" "file_sha256" "d75d4a38c83c29b580c5e9b61f9dcf379c7c0e6e7a30eb1b67da26da90587ddc"} {"question" "e-292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $g(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =g(a)+{g}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =g(4)+{g}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $g(4)=3,$ and since $g'(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ then $g'(4)=\\sqrt{4}=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+2(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $g(3.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3.6) & ≈L(3.6) \\\\  & =3+2(3.6-4) \\\\  & =3+2(-0.4) \\\\  & =3-0.8 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/example-292.json" "file_sha256" "d75d4a38c83c29b580c5e9b61f9dcf379c7c0e6e7a30eb1b67da26da90587ddc"} {"question" "e-293" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,-5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0).$ If $f'(-2)=3$, estimate the value of $f(-2.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/example-293.json" "file_sha256" "2f2582079f4f107589437c3525d60e66f5d9814989cc78b90e0a248690af3d4d"} {"question" "e-293" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-2,-5),$ so we have $f(-2)=-5.$ Also, we're told that $f'(-2)=3,$ so we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-5+3(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.3) & ≈L(-2.3) \\\\  & =-5+3(-2.3+2) \\\\  & =-5+3(-0.3) \\\\  & =-5-0.9 \\\\  & =-5.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$, the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-2,$ and therefore we have an overestimate." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/example-293.json" "file_sha256" "2f2582079f4f107589437c3525d60e66f5d9814989cc78b90e0a248690af3d4d"} {"question" "q-47782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(2.2)≈2.8,$ an underestimate"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "b386c91ff220a94a9cbaa77c201102c762e0bca829591481902130687a7f6625" "reviewer_session" "01a108cd-e7f4-73f3-b8fc-c82135bc6b7d" "rationale" "The tangent approximation is L(x)=3−(x−2), so L(2.2)=2.8. Concavity upward places the tangent below the graph, making this an underestimate. The original question, authentic worked solution, and saved Correct grade establish option e (original index 4). The stored key expresses the same answer using \\approx and placing the comma outside the math delimiters." "bindings" {"problem" "558013348dc2b3b2b004ba20134f94b90a712715479fee7a1c0ab1056012c387" "worked_solution" "c64d78016f5ef42e17d26c6ecb34e0a4c1a5675449033c0e73c8844a931ca145" "original_field" "7d4bd3c7d2582317fd37f4e2738dab8f544749b28a1e16d5e9cbdf7134dc3582" "reviewed_field" "fcbce3d2d1c90ef2b7f09b29df8af383cac3eeb2ba2a21e376714522914cb48c" "raw_widget" "f67ba292ecea6cb84cfa067068b15dba8e59084d53e2d169cfa70b888c4a6a4d"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-47782-before.png" "sha256" "1445d6423cdaee5fad41aa11f92dc9f23225abf8f29c2fbff2778ebc8b253bc4"} {"path" "history-q-47782.png" "sha256" "374ca3e60f93d48cdbd535a30797ddf41ce4d143258aac879e648f29c89d925c"} {"path" "q-47782-after.json" "sha256" "fe97d9423974140d70244239741e88bf002a9278b5a5c894492ef488b6895746"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "3f0286c13ab5d27ce4a7876f29f1088c2b89dd95cb9e878b61ec64292b7de3d6"}]} {"question" "q-47782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(2.2)≈2.8,$ an overestimate"] ["text" "$f(2.2)≈2.8,$ an underestimate"] ["text" "$f(2.2)≈2.9,$ an underestimate"] ["text" "$f(2.2)≈3.2,$ an overestimate"] ["text" "$f(2.2)≈3.2,$ an underestimate"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029603/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "b386c91ff220a94a9cbaa77c201102c762e0bca829591481902130687a7f6625" "rationale" "The tangent approximation is L(x)=3−(x−2), so L(2.2)=2.8. Concavity upward places the tangent below the graph, making this an underestimate. The original question, authentic worked solution, and saved Correct grade establish option e (original index 4). The stored key expresses the same answer using \\approx and placing the comma outside the math delimiters."}] "usage" {"e-291" [] "q-47782" [] "e-292" [] "e-293" [] "q-4804" []} "basis" 1199 "retractions" [[17592186078893 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nLet $f$ be a function such that $f^{′}(x) = x^{2} - 4$ and $f(4) = 13$. Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.5)$."] [17592186078885 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ passes through $(2, 3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0, 3)$. If $f^{′}(2) =-1$, estimate the value of $f(2.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate."] [17592186078885 :question/answer-fields 17592186076521] [17592186078154 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(-2) = 0$ and $f'(-2)=2$. Estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$."] [17592186078154 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x - (-2)) \\\\\n&= 0 + 2(x + 2) \\\\\n&= 2(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8$. We get\n\n$$\nf(-1.8) \\mid \\approx L(-1.8); = 2(-1.8 + 2); = 2(0.2); = 0.4\n$$"] [17592186078155 :question/problem "Let $g$ be a function such that $g'(x) =\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(4) = 3$. Using the tangent line approximation of $g(x)$ at $x=4$, estimate the value of $g(3.6)$."] [17592186078155 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $g(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= g(a) + g^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= g(4) + g^{′}(4)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $g(4)=3$, and since\n\n$$\ng'(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ng'(4)=\\sqrt{4}=2\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 3 + 2(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $g(3.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.6$. We get\n\n$$\ng(3.6) \\mid \\approx L(3.6); = 3 + 2(3.6 - 4); = 3 + 2(-0.4); = 3 - 0.8; = 2.2\n$$"] [17592186078159 :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,-5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$. If $f'(-2)=3$, estimate the value of $f(-2.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate."] [17592186078159 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x - (-2)) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-2,-5)$, so we have\n\n$$\nf(-2)=-5\n$$\n\nAlso, we're told that $f'(-2)=3$, so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= -5 + 3(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.3$. We get\n\n$$\nf(-2.3) \\mid \\approx L(-2.3); =-5 + 3(-2.3 + 2); =-5 + 3(-0.3); =-5 - 0.9; =-5.9\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$, the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-2$, and therefore we have an overestimate."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186076522 17592186076523 17592186076524 17592186076525 17592186076526 17592186076570 17592186076571 17592186076572 17592186076573 17592186076574] "immutable_fields" [[17592186076521 :answer-field/correct] [17592186076521 :answer-field/id] [17592186076521 :answer-field/key] [17592186076521 :answer-field/type] [17592186076569 :answer-field/correct] [17592186076569 :answer-field/id] [17592186076569 :answer-field/key] [17592186076569 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
